t can be generated by a given formal grammar.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Sense; meaning; significance.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Noght o word spak he moore than was neede, / And that was seyd in forme and reverence / And short and quyk and ful of hy sentence […]", "reference": "[1387–1400, Geoffrey Chaucer, “The Prologues”, in The Canterbury Tales (in Middle English), [Westminster: William Caxton, published 1478], →OCLC; republished in [William Thynne], editor, The Workes of Geffray Chaucer Newlye Printed, […], [London]: […] [Richard Grafton for] Iohn Reynes […], 1542, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "now to the discourse itself, voluble enough, and full of sentence, but that, for the most part, either specious rather than solid, or to his cause nothing pertinent.", "reference": "1649, J[ohn] Milton, ΕΙΚΟΝΟΚΛΆΣΤΗΣ [Eikonoklástēs] […], London: […] Matthew Simmons, […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "Full of high sentence, but a bit obtuse; […]", "reference": "1915 June, T[homas] S[tearns] Eliot, “The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock”, in Prufrock and Other Observations, London: The Egoist […], published 1917, →OCLC, page 15:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) One's opinion; manner of thinking.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "My sentence is for open war.", "reference": "1667, John Milton, “Book II”, in Paradise Lost. […], London: […] [Samuel Simmons], and are to be sold by Peter Parker […]; [a]nd by Robert Boulter […]; [a]nd Matthias Walker, […], →OCLC; republished as Paradise Lost in Ten Books: […], London: Basil Montagu Pickering […], 1873, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(archaic) A pronounced opinion or judgment on a given question.", "tags": ["archaic"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "[I]f it may bee lawfull to iudge or giue any ſentence thereof, it [the author of the book of Ruth] was either Samuell, or ſome other godly Prophet vnder the raigne of Saule, [...]", "reference": "1596, Edward Topsell, The Reward of Religion: Deliuered in Sundry Lectures Vpon the Booke of Ruth : Wherein the Godly May See Their Dayly Both Inward and Outward Trials : with the Presence of God to Assist Them, and His Mercies to Recompence Them …, London: John Windet, published 1601, page 1:"}, {"text": "By them [Martin Luther's works] we might pass sentence upon his doctrines.", "reference": "1687, Francis Atterbury, An Answer to Some Considerations on the Spirit of Martin Luther and the Original of the Reformation; […], Oxford, Oxfordshire: […] [Sheldonian] Theater, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["verdict", "conviction"], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈsɛn.təns/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈsɛntn̩(t)s]", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈsɛnʔn̩(t)s]", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "sentences", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "sentencing", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "sentenced", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "sentenced", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive) To declare a sentence on a convicted person; to condemn to punishment.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["The judge sentenced the embezzler to ten years in prison, along with a hefty fine.", "1900, Charles W. Chesnutt, The House Behind the Cedars, Chapter I,\nThe murderer, he recalled, had been tried and sentenced to imprisonment for life, but was pardoned by a merciful governor after serving a year of his sentence."], "quotes": [{"text": "Nature herself is sentenced in your doom.", "reference": "1675, Joh